@quincy_letter_support
view-if: quincy_support == 1

Dear Bruce,

I'm not much for fancy words. Never was, no matter how many speeches my staff wrote for me. So I'll say this plain.

You won. Fair and square. And for the first time  years, I'm not afraid of what comes next. Because it's you.

I remember the night when Bolton went buck-wild. I was alone in this office. The phones were dead. The National Guard wasn't answering. And I sat right here, in this chair, a cigar in my mouth and not a clue what to do. Then my secretary buzzed and said, "Bruce Wayne is on line two." I almost laughed. I thought you were calling about another permits. Something about building permits. Something like that.

But you weren't soft. You said, "Mayor, I've got Batman on the line. Say the word, he will do what needs to be done." And you meant it. You didn't ask for credit. You didn't leak it to the press. You just showed up. He just showed up and Bolton got locked up.

When the Arkham budget got frozen and I couldn't pay for the night staff, you wrote a check before I could finish explaining the problem. When my own deputy tried to stab me in the back, you were the one who told me to keep my chin up. You said, "Quincy, you're not crazy. You're just tired." I've never forgotten that.

I know people whisper about me. Say I'm out of touch. Say I held on too long. Maybe they're right. But they don't know the half of it. They don't know the nights I sat here praying for someone to help, and the only person who ever walked through that door was you. Not the council. Not the commissioner. You.

So this letter, It's a thank you.
The city isn't a machine, Bruce. It's a patient. I've been trying to keep it stable for a long time, but I'm not a young man anymore, and I'm not a fool. I know my limits. You don't have the same limits I do. You have resources. You have people who love you. And more than that, you have a good heart. You hide it behind all that money and that square jaw, but I've seen it. I've leaned on it.

Take care of this place. Take care of the people at the bottom. The ones who don't vote, don't donate, don't show up in the society pages. They're the ones who need a mayor who sees them. You'll see them. I know you will. Just never share this same kindness to people on the same level as yours. For me, for them. It's just weakness. I'm giving it back to you now because I've given nothing to you or anybody else.

The desk is yours. The office is yours. And my respect, that's yours too, has been for years.

Come visit sometime. I'll make tea, we'll share a smoke. The good kind, not the zoning meeting chamomile I told you about last time.

With gratitude,

Quincy Sharp

P.S. The lock on the private washroom has been temperamental for a while. You probably can guess as to why. Give it a firm jiggle. 

- @redirect1: At the end of the day, Quincy was simply a man who could not put up the fight that was being asked of him. Nevertheless, the war continues.
- @redirect2: Rest easy now, Quincy. I'll take on the mantle and make this city safe, old friend.
- @redirect3: As necessary as our partnership was, I can't say I share the same sentiment. He ruined a lot of people's lives.
- @redirect4: Old Quincy. No self awareness and no self reflection. I can finally put this behind me.

