@quincy_letter
view-if: quincy_support == 0

My Dearest Bruce,

First, let me extend my warmest congratulations. To defeat an incumbent of my stature — a man who single-handedly redefined this city's moral architecture — is no small feat. I tip my cap to you, sir. You ran a spirited campaign. Energetic. Youthful. A bit loud with the posters, perhaps, but spirited nonetheless.

I must confess, I was rather surprised by the outcome. Polling, as you know, is an inexact science. My intern (a very bright young Woman from Gotham Academy) assured me the numbers were "quite favorable." She showed me a chart. A very colorful chart. I assume he misread it. These things happen. No hard feelings.

As I prepare to vacate this office, I have taken the liberty of leaving for you a comprehensive transition binder. It is twelve volumes long, bound in genuine leather, and contains every important thought I have ever had about running a city. You will find my thoughts on ribbon colors for police commendations, the correct brand of chamomile tea for zoning hearings, and a complete list of acceptable floral arrangements for the mayor's foyer (no carnations — I will not elaborate).

Regarding the city's operational matters — the budgets, the crime statistics, the something-or-other about the sewers that my deputy kept mentioning — I am told your people will speak to my people. I have never fully understood what happens in those meetings. I assume it gets handled. It always has.

A few small housekeeping notes:

 The mayor's private washroom is the third door on the left. The lock is temperamental. Give it a firm jiggle.
 My portrait is already hung in the east corridor. I trust you will not move it. It is load-bearing.
 If the Arkham board calls, tell them I am "in a meeting." They will understand.

You will find that the job is mostly ceremonial, really. Smiling at parades. Cutting ribbons. Occasionally declaring a "State of Emergency" when the trains don't run on time. The heavy lifting is done by people whose names I have forgotten. You will forget them too. It is part of the job's unique charm.

I leave this office with my head held high, my legacy secure, and my full belief that you will not break anything too expensive. Try not to embarrass the position. It took me too long to build its dignity.

Warmly,

Quincy Sharp

Mayor of Gotham City

P.S. The key to the executive humidor is taped under the globe. Do not smoke the cigars from the left drawer. Those are mine. I am taking them. If you take them, I AM going to take you to court over this, I'm warning you.

P.P.S. Who is your tailor? That suit is remarkable. 

